        description:
          'After 20 requests, 429 Too Many Requests error: Gemini free quota reached. Log analysis to understand.',
      },
      {
        step: 'Step 06',
        title: 'Code generation',
        description:
          "Ambitious test: 'Create a Flappy Bird game'. The agent generated the complete HTML/CSS/JS, playable in the browser.",
      },
      {
        step: 'Step 07',
        title: 'Monitoring',
        description:
          'Viewing Stats (CPU, RAM, disk) and Logs (API calls, errors). Files section to see creations.',
      },
    ]}
    flow={[
      {
        num: 1,
        title: 'User request',
        text: 'The user sends a message in the Agent Zero web interface.',
      },
      {
        num: 2,
        title: 'Agent processing',
        text: 'Agent Zero orchestrates the request, decides on actions (read file, code, etc.).',
      },
      {
        num: 3,
        title: 'Model call',
        text: 'The agent sends context to the Gemini model via Google API.',
      },
      {
        num: 4,
        title: 'Response and action',
        text: 'Gemini returns the response, the agent displays it or executes the action (creates a file, for example).',
      },
    ]}
    architecture="Browser → Agent Zero Web Interface (Docker, port 80) → Google Gemini API → Response displayed in chat"
    metrics={[
      { value: 'Docker', label: 'Containerization' },
      { value: 'Gemini', label: 'AI model' },
      { value: '20/day', label: 'Free quota' },
      { value: '1 wk', label: 'Project duration' },
    ]}
    repo="https://github.com/Flavino-Hack/agent-zero-project"
  >
    <h2>Context</h2>
    <p>
      After deploying my own AI assistant with <strong>OpenClaw</strong>, I wanted to explore
      another framework: <strong>Agent Zero</strong>. Unlike OpenClaw (a personal assistant), Agent
      Zero is an <strong>autonomous agent</strong> capable of creating files, writing code and
      interacting with its environment.
    </p>
    <p>The goal was threefold:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Deploy</strong> Agent Zero in a Docker container on Kali Linux
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Configure</strong> access to AI models (OpenRouter, Google Gemini)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Test</strong> code generation capabilities and autonomy
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <p>The system relies on a simple but complete stack:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        A <strong>Docker</strong> container hosts Agent Zero, the Flask API and services (managed by
        Supervisor)
      </li>
      <li>
        The user accesses the <strong>web interface</strong> on the mapped port (32769 by default)
      </li>
      <li>
        Agent Zero communicates with <strong>Google Gemini</strong> via API to generate responses
      </li>
      <li>
        The agent can <strong>create files</strong> (games, scripts) that persist in the container
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Implementation steps</h2>

    <h3>1. Docker container launch</h3>
    <p>
      Image <code>frdel/agent-zero-run:latest</code>, exposing ports 80 (web interface) and 22
      (SSH). All services managed by Supervisor.
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker run -d -p 32769:80 -p 32768:22 frdel/agent-zero-run:latest</code></pre>

    <h3>2. OpenRouter configuration (failure)</h3>
    <p>
      First test with OpenRouter, thinking to use free models.{' '}
      <strong>Error 402 Payment Required</strong>: free models have been removed, payment is now
      required.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      "This request requires more credits, or fewer max_tokens. You requested up to 65536 tokens,
      but can only afford 5000."
    </blockquote>

    <h3>3. Migration to Google Gemini</h3>
    <p>
      Switching provider: Google Gemini offers a <strong>free tier</strong> with a quota of{' '}
      <strong>20 requests/day</strong>. Free API key obtained via Google AI Studio, model{' '}
      <code>gemini-3.6-flash</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Configuring the 3 models</h3>
    <p>Agent Zero uses 3 models:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Chat Model</strong>: the main model, for conversations
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Utility Model</strong>: for secondary tasks (summarization, memory)
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Web Browser Model</strong>: for built-in web navigation
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      All 3 configured on <code>gemini-3.6-flash</code> to stay within quotas.
    </p>

    <h3>5. First success 🎉</h3>
    <p>
      The test with a simple "Hello" works. The agent responds correctly. Gemini configuration is
      validated.
    </p>

    <h3>6. Limit reached</h3>
    <p>
      After a few tests, <strong>429 Too Many Requests error</strong>: the 20 requests/day quota is
      exhausted.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      "Quota exceeded for metric:
      generativelanguage.googleapis.com/generate_content_free_tier_requests, limit: 20"
    </blockquote>

    <h3>7. Ambitious test — Flappy Bird</h3>
    <p>
      Instruction: <em>"Create a Flappy Bird game"</em>. The agent generated{' '}
      <strong>complete HTML, CSS and JavaScript</strong>, with a real game playable in the browser.
      A test that demonstrates the agent's power.
    </p>

    <h2>Challenges encountered</h2>

    <h3>🚫 OpenRouter paid</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: 402 error, free models have been removed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: migration to Google Gemini, free with daily quota.
    </p>

    <h3>⏱️ Gemini quota reached</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: 429 error after 20 requests.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solutions</strong>: wait 24h, add a card on Google Cloud, use multiple accounts, or
      switch to local Ollama.
    </p>

    <h3>💾 Container memory</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Problem</strong>: Docker limits memory by default. The agent may crash with too much
      context.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Solution</strong>: Stats monitoring, context token adjustment, automatic container
      restart.
    </p>

    <h2>Result</h2>
    <p>
      The deployment is <strong>functional</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>✅ Agent Zero runs in a Docker container on Kali Linux</li>
      <li>✅ Web interface accessible and responsive</li>
      <li>✅ Google Gemini configured and functional (within quotas)</li>
      <li>✅ Code generation: complete and playable Flappy Bird game</li>
      <li>✅ Monitoring: built-in Stats and Logs</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What I take away</h2>
    <p>
      This project taught me <strong>three things</strong>:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Free AI models are ephemeral</strong> — OpenRouter removed its free models, Gemini
        limits to 20 requests/day. For intensive use, you need a local model (Ollama) or a paid
        account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Autonomous agents are powerful</strong> — creating a complete game from a sentence
        is impressive. But each call costs tokens.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Monitoring is essential</strong> — without Stats and Logs, it's impossible to
        diagnose quota or memory errors.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Improvement paths</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Local model</strong>: install Ollama + Llama 3 or Mistral, no quota
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>VPS server</strong>: host Agent Zero on a VPS with custom domain
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Authentication</strong>: add a login to protect the interface
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Integrations</strong>: connect the agent to tools (email, calendar)
      </li>
    </ul>
  </ProjectLayout>
</BaseLayout>
